#: ../../../users/relaysrv.rst:4
msgid "Custom Relay Server"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/relaysrv.rst:6
msgid ""
"Syncthing relies on a network of community-contributed relay servers. Anyone "
"can run a relay server, and it will automatically join the relay pool and be "
"available to Syncthing users. The current list of relays can be found at "
"https://relays.syncthing.net."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/relaysrv.rst:11
msgid ""
"To run a relay of your own, you will first need a server. Running a relay "
"from a home computer is not recommended: the relay ideally needs to be "
"running 24/7, and home internet connections often have poor upload bandwidth."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/relaysrv.rst:15
msgid "The recommended specifications for a relay are:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/relaysrv.rst:18
msgid "CPU"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/relaysrv.rst:18
msgid "At least 1GHz"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/relaysrv.rst:19
msgid "RAM"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/relaysrv.rst:19
msgid "At least 256MB"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/relaysrv.rst:20
msgid "Bandwidth"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/relaysrv.rst:20
msgid "At least 1MBit/s in and out, the higher the better"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/relaysrv.rst:21
msgid "Traffic"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/relaysrv.rst:21
msgid "At least 1TiB/month, the more the better"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/relaysrv.rst:24
msgid "VPS's with these specs are available for about $5-$10/month."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/relaysrv.rst:27
msgid "Installing and Running"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/relaysrv.rst:29
msgid ""
"Download the latest release of the `relay server <https://github.com/"
"syncthing/relaysrv/releases>`__ for your operating system and architecture. "
"Unpack the archive and save the binary to a convenient place such as `/usr/"
"local/bin`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/relaysrv.rst:33
msgid ""
"The relay server takes a number of options, some of which are important for "
"smooth operation::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/relaysrv.rst:36
msgid ""
"$ relaysrv --help\n"
"Usage of relaysrv:\n"
"  -debug\n"
"        Enable debug output\n"
"  -ext-address string\n"
"        An optional address to advertising as being available on.\n"
"        Allows listening on an unprivileged port with port forwarding from e."
"g.\n"
"        443, and be connected to on port 443.\n"
"  -global-rate int\n"
"        Global rate limit, in bytes/s\n"
"  -keys string\n"
"        Directory where cert.pem and key.pem is stored (default \".\")\n"
"  -listen string\n"
"        Protocol listen address (default \":22067\")\n"
"  -message-timeout duration\n"
"        Maximum amount of time we wait for relevant messages to arrive "
"(default 1m0s)\n"
"  -network-timeout duration\n"
"        Timeout for network operations between the client and the relay.\n"
"        If no data is received between the client and the relay in this\n"
"        period of time, the connection is terminated. Furthermore, if no\n"
"        data is sent between either clients being relayed within this\n"
"        period of time, the session is also terminated. (default 2m0s)\n"
"  -per-session-rate int\n"
"        Per session rate limit, in bytes/s\n"
"  -ping-interval duration\n"
"        How often pings are sent (default 1m0s)\n"
"  -pools string\n"
"        Comma separated list of relay pool addresses to join (default "
"\"https://relays.syncthing.net/endpoint\")\n"
"  -provided-by string\n"
"        An optional description about who provides the relay\n"
"  -status-srv string\n"
"        Listen address for status service (blank to disable) (default "
"\":22070\")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/relaysrv.rst:69
msgid ""
"Primarily, you need to decide on a directory to store the TLS key and "
"certificate and a listen port. The default listen port of 22067 works, but "
"for optimal compatibility a well known port for encrypted traffic such as "
"443 is recommended. This may require additional setup to work without "
"running as root or a privileged user, see `Running on port 443 as an "
"unprivileged user`_ below. In principle something similar to this should "
"work on a Linux/Unix system::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/relaysrv.rst:77
msgid ""
"$ sudo useradd relaysrv\n"
"$ sudo mkdir /etc/relaysrv\n"
"$ sudo chown relaysrv /etc/relaysrv\n"
"$ sudo -u relaysrv /usr/local/bin/relaysrv -keys /etc/relaysrv"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/relaysrv.rst:82
msgid ""
"This creates a user ``relaysrv`` and a directory ``/etc/relaysrv`` to store "
"the keys. The keys are generated on first startup. The relay will join the "
"global relay pool, unless a ``-pools=\"\"`` argument is given."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/relaysrv.rst:86
msgid ""
"To make the relay server start automatically at boot, use the recommended "
"procedure for your operating system."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/relaysrv.rst:90
msgid "Running on port 443 as an unprivileged user"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/relaysrv.rst:92
msgid ""
"It is recommended that you run the relay on port 443 (or another port which "
"is commonly allowed through corporate firewalls), in order to maximise the "
"chances that people are able to connect. However, binding to ports below "
"1024 requires root privileges, and running a relay as root is not "
"recommended. Thankfully there are a couple of approaches available to you."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/relaysrv.rst:98
msgid ""
"One option is to run the relay on port 22067, and use an ``iptables`` rule "
"to forward traffic from port 443 to port 22067, for example::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/relaysrv.rst:101
msgid ""
"iptables -t nat -A PREROUTING -i eth0 -p tcp --dport 443 -j REDIRECT --to-"
"port 22067"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/relaysrv.rst:103
msgid ""
"Or, if you're using ``ufw``, add the following to ``/etc/ufw/before.rules``::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/relaysrv.rst:105
msgid ""
"*nat\n"
":PREROUTING ACCEPT [0:0]\n"
":POSTROUTING ACCEPT [0:0]\n"
"\n"
"-A PREROUTING -i eth0 -p tcp --dport 443 -j REDIRECT --to-port 22067\n"
"\n"
"COMMIT"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/relaysrv.rst:113
msgid ""
"You will need to start ``relaysrv`` with ``-ext-address \":443\"``. This "
"tells ``relaysrv`` that it can be contacted on port 443, even though it is "
"listening on port 22067. You will also need to let both port 443 and 22067 "
"through your firewall."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../users/relaysrv.rst:118
msgid ""
"Another option is `described here <https://wiki.apache.org/httpd/"
"NonRootPortBinding>`__, although your milage may vary."
msgstr ""
